{{Draft topics|biography|east-asia}}
{{AfC topic|bdp}}

{{draft}}
{{promising draft}}
{{Expand Japanese|ニギハヤヒ}}{{Infobox deity
| type = Shinto
| image = File:饒速日尊の墳墓.jpg
| caption = Tomb of Nigihayahi[[fr:Nigihayahi]][[ja:ニギハヤヒ]]
| father = [[Ame-no-oshihomimi]]<ref name="kokugakuin" />
}}
'''Nigihayahi''' is a Japanese deity.<ref name="kokugakuin">{{Cite web |date=2023-03-21 |title=Nigihayahi {{!}} 國學院大學デジタルミュージアム |url=https://d-museum.kokugakuin.ac.jp/eos/detail/?id=9356 |access-date=2023-12-05 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230321165541/https://d-museum.kokugakuin.ac.jp/eos/detail/?id=9356 |archive-date=2023-03-21 }}</ref> mentioned in the [[Kojiki]], [[Kujiki]], and [[Nihon Shoki]]<ref name="Como-2009a">{{Cite book |last=Como |first=Michael |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=-UNEhdX6ePMC&dq=nigihayahi&pg=PA169 |title=Weaving and Binding: Immigrant Gods and Female Immortals in Ancient Japan |date=2009-09-02 |publisher=University of Hawaii Press |isbn=978-0-8248-2957-5 |language=en}}</ref>

He was involved with the [[Tenson Korin]].<ref name="Satomi-2013a">{{Cite book |last=Satomi |first=Kishio |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=uWem9l1u-9cC&dq=nigihayahi&pg=PA109 |title=Discovery of Japanese Idealism |date=2013-07-04 |publisher=Routledge |isbn=978-1-136-39365-5 |language=en}}</ref> The [[Mononobe clan]] claimed him as their ancestor.<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Cali |first1=Joseph |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=J1gEEAAAQBAJ&dq=nigihayahi&pg=PA159 |title=Shinto Shrines: A Guide to the Sacred Sites of Japan's Ancient Religion |last2=Dougill |first2=John |date=2012-11-30 |publisher=University of Hawaii Press |isbn=978-0-8248-3775-4 |language=en}}</ref>

In ''[[Kujiki]]'', [[Amenohoakari]] is considered the same ''kami'' as {{Interlanguage link|Nigihayahi|ja|ニギハヤヒ}}, the ancestral god of the Hozumi and [[Mononobe clan|Mononobe]] clans, but this contradicts their generational relationships and areas of activity.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Hoga |first=Toshio |title=Shoki Mononobe-uji no Keifu (1) Kokigi no Heya (初期物部氏の系譜 (1) 古樹紀之房間) |year=2007 |location=Japan}}</ref>

He is sometimes described as the older brother of [[Ninigi]]<ref>{{Cite book |last=Barnes |first=Gina |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=tn1IpE-YZOQC&dq=nigihayahi&pg=PA153 |title=State Formation in Japan: Emergence of a 4th-Century Ruling Elite |date=2007-03-12 |publisher=Routledge |isbn=978-1-134-38469-3 |language=en}}</ref>, or the son of [[Ame-no-oshihomimi]].<ref name="kokugakuin" />

The nature of the deity and its myths in the [[Kojiki]], [[Kujiki]], and [[Nihon Shoki]] all suggest a possible Korean influence.<ref name="Como-2009a" />

