le.com/books?id=q540U2rrWKcC&dq=%22Itsuse%22&pg=PA363 |title=The Rise and Fall of the Japanese Empire |date=2010-11-01 |publisher=Routledge |isbn=978-1-136-92547-4 |language=en}}</ref><ref name=":0">{{Cite book |last=Various |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=P1mLEAAAQBAJ&dq=nigihayahi&pg=PT19 |title=Japan: From the Japanese Government History |date=2022-09-16 |publisher=DigiCat |language=en}}</ref> Nagasunehiko claimed to follow Nigihayahi.<ref name="kokugakuin" /><ref name=":0" /> Jimmu realized that they had been defeated because they battled eastward against the sun, so he decided to land on the east side of [[Kii Peninsula]] and to battle westward. They reached [[Kumano Region|Kumano]], and, with the guidance of a [[three-legged crow]], ''Yatagarasu'' ("eight-span crow"), they moved to [[Yamato Province|Yamato]]. There, they once again battled Nagasunehiko and were victorious.<ref name="Iwate">{{cite web |date=September 24, 2004 |script-title=ja:朝廷軍の侵略に抵抗 |url=http://www.iwate-np.co.jp/sekai/sekaiisan/sekaiisan6.htm |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160303180753/http://www.iwate-np.co.jp/sekai/sekaiisan/sekaiisan6.htm |archive-date=March 3, 2016 |access-date=March 1, 2011 |publisher=[[Iwate Nippo]] |language=ja}}</ref> Nigihayahi killed Nagasunehiko and submitted to Jimmu.<ref name="kokugakuin" /><ref name=":0" />
