oks?id=q540U2rrWKcC&dq=%22Itsuse%22&pg=PA363 |title=The Rise and Fall of the Japanese Empire |date=2010-11-01 |publisher=Routledge |isbn=978-1-136-92547-4 |language=en}}</ref><ref name=":0">{{Cite book |last=Various |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=P1mLEAAAQBAJ&dq=nigihayahi&pg=PT19 |title=Japan: From the Japanese Government History |date=2022-09-16 |publisher=DigiCat |language=en}}</ref> Nagasunehiko claimed to follow Nigihayahi.<ref name="kokugakuin" /><ref name=":0" /> Jimmu realized that they had been defeated because they battled eastward against the sun, so he decided to land on the east side of [[Kii Peninsula]] and to battle westward. They reached [[Kumano Region|Kumano]], and, with the guidance of a [[three-legged crow]], ''Yatagarasu'' ("eight-span crow"), they moved to [[Yamato Province|Yamato]]. There, they once again battled Nagasunehiko and were victorious.<ref name="Iwate">{{cite web |date=September 24, 2004 |script-title=ja:朝廷軍の侵略に抵抗 |url=http://www.iwate-np.co.jp/sekai/sekaiisan/sekaiisan6.htm |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160303180753/http://www.iwate-np.co.jp/sekai/sekaiisan/sekaiisan6.htm |archive-date=March 3, 2016 |access-date=March 1, 2011 |publisher=[[Iwate Nippo]] |language=ja}}</ref> Nigihayahi killed Nagasunehiko and submitted to Jimmu.<ref name="kokugakuin" /><ref name=":0" />
In Yamato, [[Nigihayahi]], who also claimed descent from the Takamagahara gods, was protected by Nagasunehiko. However, when Nigihayahi met Jimmu, he accepted Jimmu's legitimacy. At this point, Jimmu is said to have ascended to the throne of Japan.<ref>[https://www.kusuyama.jp/culture/tombo-dragonfly/ メンテナンス中]</ref>
== In popular culture == He is present in [[Ghost of Tsushima]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-08-21 |title=Charm Of Nigihayahi-No-Mikoto - Location & Effects {{!}} Ghost Of Tsushima |url=https://gamewith.net/ghost-of-tsushima/article/show/20521 |access-date=2023-12-05 |website=GameWith |language=en-US}}</ref>
