'''Nigihayahi''' is a Japanese deity.<ref name="kokugakuin">{{Cite web |date=2023-03-21 |title=Nigihayahi {{!}} 國學院大學デジタルミュージアム |url=https://d-museum.kokugakuin.ac.jp/eos/detail/?id=9356 |access-date=2023-12-05 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230321165541/https://d-museum.kokugakuin.ac.jp/eos/detail/?id=9356 |archive-date=2023-03-21 }}</ref> mentioned in the [[Kojiki]], {{ill|Kujiki|en|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kujiki|qq=}}, and [[Nihon Shoki]]<ref name="Como-2009a">{{Cite book|last=Como |first=Michael |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=-UNEhdX6ePMC&dq=nigihayahi&pg=PA169 |title=Weaving and Binding: Immigrant Gods and Female Immortals in Ancient Japan |date=2009-09-02 |publisher=University of Hawaii Press |isbn=978-0-8248-2957-5 |language=en|qq=draft}}</ref>

He was involved with the [[Tenson kōrin|Tenson Korin]].<ref name="Satomi-2013a">{{Cite book |last=Satomi |first=Kishio |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=uWem9l1u-9cC&dq=nigihayahi&pg=PA109 |title=Discovery of Japanese Idealism |date=2013-07-04 |publisher=Routledge |isbn=978-1-136-39365-5 |language=en}}</ref> The [[Mononobe clan]] claimed him as their ancestor.<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Cali |first1=Joseph |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=J1gEEAAAQBAJ&dq=nigihayahi&pg=PA159 |title=Shinto Shrines: A Guide to the Sacred Sites of Japan's Ancient Religion |last2=Dougill |first2=John |date=2012-11-30 |publisher=University of Hawaii Press |isbn=978-0-8248-3775-4 |language=en}}</ref>

In the {{ill|Kujiki|en|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kujiki|qq=}}, {{ill|Amenohoakari|simple|User:Immanuelle/Amenohoakari|fr|User:Immanuelle/Amenohoakari|en|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Amenohoakari|qq=}} is considered the same ''kami'' as {{ill|Nigihayahi|ja|ニギハヤヒ|fr|6=en:draft|8=simple|9=User:Immanuelle/Nigihayahi|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Nigihayahi|qq=}}, the ancestral god of the Hozumi and [[Mononobe clan|Mononobe]] clans, but this contradicts their generational relationships and areas of activity.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Hoga |first=Toshio |title=Shoki Mononobe-uji no Keifu (1) Kokigi no Heya (初期物部氏の系譜 (1) 古樹紀之房間) |year=2007 |location=Japan}}</ref>

He is sometimes described as the older brother of [[Ninigi-no-Mikoto|Ninigi]]<ref>{{Cite book |last=Barnes |first=Gina |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=tn1IpE-YZOQC&dq=nigihayahi&pg=PA153 |title=State Formation in Japan: Emergence of a 4th-Century Ruling Elite |date=2007-03-12 |publisher=Routledge |isbn=978-1-134-38469-3 |language=en}}</ref>, or the son of [[:en:Ame-no-oshihomimi|Ame-no-oshihomimi]].<ref name="kokugakuin" />

The nature of the deity and its myths in the [[Kojiki]], {{ill|Kujiki|en|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kujiki|qq=}}, and [[Nihon Shoki]] all suggest a possible Korean influence.<ref name="Como-2009a" />

== Jimmu's Eastern Expedition ==
During [[Jimmu's Eastern Expedition]] Jimmu and his brothers reached Naniwa (modern-day [[:en:Osaka|Osaka]]), they encountered another local chieftain, ''Nagasunehiko'' ("the long-legged man"), and [[Itsuse no Mikoto|Itsuse]] was killed in the ensuing battle<ref>{{Cite book |last=Bunko (Japan) |first=Tōyō |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=sZnWAAAAMAAJ&q=%22Itsuse%22 |title=Memoirs of the Research Department |date=1975 |language=en|qq=draft}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2023-03-21 |title=Kamuyamatoiwarebiko {{!}} 國學院大學デジタルミュージアム |url=https://d-museum.kokugakuin.ac.jp/eos/detail/?id=9379 |access-date=2023-11-23 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230321165628/https://d-museum.kokugakuin.ac.jp/eos/detail/?id=9379 |archive-date=2023-03-21 }}</ref> .<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Monbushō |first1=Japan |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=wXoLAAAAIAAJ&dq=%22Itsuse%22&pg=PA24 |title=History of the Empire of Japan |last2=Brinkley |first2=FRank |date=1893 |publisher=Dai Nippon Tosho Kabushiki Kwaisha, by order of the Department of Education. Printed at the Japan Mail Office, Yokohama |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Roberts |first=Jeremy |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=xdfgjV2kw6oC&dq=%22Itsuse%22&pg=PA63 |title=Japanese Mythology A to Z |date=2009 |publisher=Infobase Publishing |isbn=978-1-4381-2802-3 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Anonymous |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=YDXaEAAAQBAJ&dq=%22Itsuse%22&pg=PA148 |title=The Great Events: Vol. 1 |date=2023-09-29 |publisher=BoD – Books on Demand |isbn=978-3-368-19823-7 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Weiss |first=David |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=nLnMEAAAQBAJ&dq=%22Itsuse%22&pg=PA99 |title=The God Susanoo and Korea in Japan's Cultural Memory: Ancient Myths and Modern Empire |date=2022-01-13 |publisher=Bloomsbury Publishing |isbn=978-1-350-27119-7 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=James |first=David H. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=q540U2rrWKcC&dq=